103.014. The Blessed One, knowing the dispositions, latent tendencies, nature, and character of that great multitude which was so well-disposed, gave such a teaching on the Four Noble Truths that many hundreds of thousands of beings took the refuges and precepts; some attained the warmth, summit, patience, and supreme worldly <s>dharmas</s>;
103.017. some directly realized the fruit of stream-entry (<s>srotāpatti</s>), the fruit of once-returning (<s>sakṛdāgāmin</s>), and the fruit of non-returning (<s>anāgāmin</s>);
103.017. some, having gone forth, through the abandonment of all afflictions, directly realized <s>Arhatship</s>;
103.018. some planted seeds toward the great enlightenment of a disciple;
103.018. some planted seeds toward solitary enlightenment (<s>pratyekabuddhī</s>).
103.019. For the most part, that assembly was established as inclined toward the <s>Buddha</s>, devoted to the <s>Dharma</s>, and leaning toward the <s>Saṃgha</s>.
103.020. Then the Blessed One, having established that assembly as inclined toward the <s>Buddha</s>, devoted to the <s>Dharma</s>, and leaning toward the <s>Saṃgha</s>, rose from his seat and departed.

121.014. Then the Blessed One, commending the words spoken by <s>Śakra</s>, the lord of the gods, said thus: 'So it is, <s>Kauśika</s>, so it is.
121.016. Those who take refuge in the <s>Buddha</s> do not go to an evil rebirth;
121.017. Having left behind human bodies, they assume divine bodies.' //4//

139.014. At the time, <s>Ānanda</s>, when King <s>Mūrdhāta</s> ascended to the <s>Trāyastriṃśa</s> (Thirty-three) gods, a thought such as this arose: 'Oh, how wonderful that <s>Śakra</s>, the lord of the gods, invites me to share half his throne!' — the monk <s>Kāśyapa</s> at that time, in that era, was <s>Śakra</s>, the lord of the gods.
139.016. At the time, <s>Ānanda</s>, when King <s>Mūrdhāta</s> gave rise to a thought such as this: 'Let me dislodge <s>Śakra</s>, the lord of the gods, from this position and myself exercise sovereign lordship and dominion over both gods and humans' — <s>Kāśyapa</s>, the Perfectly Enlightened One, at that time, in that era, was <s>Śakra</s>, the lord of the gods.
139.019. Having generated a corrupt thought toward a being of great renown, he therefore fell from his supernatural power and landed in <s>Jambudvīpa</s>, was struck by a severe illness, and experienced a deep, death-ending pain.
139.020. He who was that King <s>Mūrdhāta</s> — I myself, <s>Ānanda</s>, was he at that time, in that era.
139.021. At that time, <s>Ānanda</s>, still possessed of passion, hatred, and delusion, still unreleased from the nature of birth, old age, sickness, death, sorrow, lamentation, suffering, grief, and despair, at the very time of death, I gave such an exhortation that many hundreds of thousands of beings, having abandoned the householder's life and gone forth among the <s>ṛṣis</s> (sages), having removed sensual desire for sensual pleasures and dwelling much in that state, were reborn in the <s>Brahma</s>-world.
139.024. Now, as the Omniscient One who has attained mastery over the unsurpassed knowledge and all that is to be known, having set forth toward <s>Nirvāṇa</s>, such a teaching of the <s>Dharma</s> has been given that many hundreds of thousands of deities have been established in the truths;
139.026. many hundreds of thousands of <s>ṛṣis</s> have been ordained with the words 'Come, monks';
139.027. by them, striving, exerting, and applying themselves, <s>Arhattva</s> (the state of an Arhat) has been attained through the abandonment of all <s>kleśas</s> (afflictions);
139.027. many gods, <s>Nāgas</s>, <s>Yakṣas</s>, <s>Gandharvas</s>, <s>Asuras</s>, <s>Garuḍas</s>, <s>Kinnaras</s>, and <s>Mahoragas</s> have been established in the refuges and precepts;
139.028. and many hundreds of thousands of beings of <s>Vaiśālī</s>, of whom some have been established in the fruit of stream-entry, some in the fruit of once-returning, some in the fruit of non-returning; by some, having gone forth, <s>Arhattva</s> has been attained; by some, the mind has been directed toward the enlightenment of a disciple, by some toward solitary enlightenment, by some toward unsurpassed perfect awakening; and by some, the refuges and precepts have been taken.

220.026. 'And moreover, sister:
220.027. Seeing forms that are fair on the outside, the fool becomes attached;
220.028. knowing them to be corrupt within, the wise one becomes detached.' //11//

221.001. <221>'Indeed, in this case:
221.002. The stench is warded off by various perfumes from this impure source; the deformity is concealed externally by various garments, ornaments, and the like;
221.004. sweat, moisture, impurities, and other filth are removed with water — yet this impure skeleton, this inauspicious thing, is served by those enslaved to desire.' //13//

262.021. <s>Kuṇāla</s> said:
262.022. 'Let death come to me, rather than while I remain established in the pure <s>Dharma</s>;
262.023. for life that is reviled by the virtuous is of no use to me.' //109//

293.001. '<s>Raṅgā</s>, <s>Pataṅgā</s>, <s>Tapanī</s>, <s>Citrā</s>, <s>Rudantī</s>, <s>Hasantī</s>, <s>Āśīviṣā</s>, and the <s>Vetra</s> River.
293.002. In <s>Raṅgā</s>, rage of <s>Rākṣasīs</s>; in <s>Pataṅgā</s>, non-human beings;
293.003. in <s>Tapanī</s>, an abundance of crocodiles; in <s>Citrā</s>, shape-shifters.' //14//

297.012. '<s>Raṅgā</s>, <s>Pataṅgā</s>, <s>Tapanī</s>, <s>Citrā</s>, <s>Rudantī</s>, <s>Hasantī</s>, <s>Āśīviṣā</s>, and the <s>Vetra</s> River.
297.013. In <s>Raṅgā</s>, there is the rage of <s>Rākṣasīs</s>; in <s>Pataṅgā</s>, non-human beings;
297.014. in <s>Tapanī</s>, a multitude of crocodiles; in <s>Citrā</s>, shape-shifters.' //38//

322.027. 'Having committed even one of the four, he becomes a non-<s>brāhmaṇa</s>;
322.028. he does not receive the proper greeting in the assembly of <s>brāhmaṇas</s>,
322.029. nor a seat, nor water, nor is he worthy of a respectful rising.' //36//

325.001. 'Whatever is fitting here, whether unequal or equal, that I shall declare to you when pressed;
325.003. and whatever fault there might be that is unjust, I shall tell you progressively;
325.005. having heard from me and reflected, O gentle one — human beings arise from the supremacy of <s>karma</s> (deeds).' //56//

333.001. '<s>Niṣadya</s> (a fisherwoman) gave birth to <s>Kālī</s>'s son, the sage <s>Dvaipāyana</s> —
333.002. fierce, radiant, terrible, possessing the five supernatural knowledges, of great austerity;
333.003. he was not the son of a <s>Brāhmaṇa</s> woman — what then will you say, O <s>Brāhmaṇa</s>?' //86//

333.004. 'The <s>Kṣatriya</s> woman named <s>Reṇukā</s> gave birth to <s>Rāma</s>, the great sage —
333.005. learned, disciplined, and versed in all <s>śāstras</s> (treatises);
333.006. he was not the son of a <s>Brāhmaṇa</s> woman — what then will you say, O <s>Brāhmaṇa</s>?' //87//

337.008. 'Now then, O <s>Puṣkarasārin</s>, I shall explain the <s>muhūrtas</s> of the night.
337.008. When the sun has set, the <s>muhūrta</s> is called <s>Raudra</s> (Fierce).
337.009. Then comes the <s>muhūrta</s> called <s>Tārāvacara</s> (Star-Wandering);
337.009. then <s>Samyama</s> (Restraint);
337.009. then <s>Sāmpraiyaka</s> (the Departing);
337.010. then <s>Ananta</s> (Endless);
337.010. then <s>Gardabha</s> (Donkey);
337.010. then <s>Rākṣasa</s> (Demonic).
337.010. At the middle of midnight, the <s>muhūrta</s> is called <s>Avayava</s> (Limb).
337.011. When midnight has passed, the <s>muhūrta</s> is called <s>Brahmā</s>;
337.011. then <s>Diti</s>;
337.011. then <s>Arka</s> (Sun-ray);
337.012. then <s>Vidhamana</s> (Scattering);
337.012. then <s>Āgneya</s> (Fiery);
337.012. then <s>Ātapāgni</s> (Blazing Fire);
337.013. then <s>Abhijit</s> (Victorious).
337.013. These are the names of the <s>muhūrtas</s> of the night.
337.013. Thus, O <s>Puṣkarasārin</s>, these are the thirty <s>muhūrtas</s> by which the day-and-night is known.'

349.008. 'The science of accountants, instrumental music, dancing, and singing;
349.009. traditional artisans, image-makers, and chariot-makers should be trained;
349.010. painters, scribes, and book-making should be done.' //169//

350.029. 'Flowers that are to be cultivated, and also roots and fruits;
350.030. gardens should be made, and charitable food-distributions should be employed.
350.031. Fierce deeds also succeed here.' //191//

For those whose ascent [normally indicates] defects, [the reverse] — from ascending, merits.
Three thousand [coins come] for [a mark on] the neck; ten [thousand] for the severing of the head;
a kingdom, or a hundred thousand, one obtains for eating [one's own] head. // 629 //

A morsel [of food] placed inside between an offering-string and a thread —
a woman who obtains this in a dream also gives birth to a son;
and if [she sees] a pair of vessels, she gives birth to twins. // 676 //

A gentle wind is praised; an excessive wind is not praised;
[seeing oneself] sick and dirty is auspicious; [seeing oneself] adorned is not praised;
ascending a mountain is auspicious; but descending from it is not. // 693 //

395.005. 'On ashes, a fruitless dispute; on sand, agitation;
395.006. at a temple gate, honor; on lotuses, great wealth;
395.007. on fruit, it is said to be in accordance with one's aim; on flowers, a meeting with a loved one.' //737//

395.022. 'According to its position and according to its color, one should predict for people.
395.023. On uneven ground with scant thickets, a persistent quarrel will occur;
395.024. on level ground in an even field, predict equal outcomes;
395.025. on a mountain-borne river, predict a journey abroad.' //745//

395.026. 'On pieces of wood, there is no great worry; on bones, however, loss of wealth;
395.027. from whichever direction the five-stepped bird approaches,
395.028. know that a journey in that direction is indicated, as it is for it, so also for the person.' //746//

396.005. 'The wise King <s>Triśaṅku</s>, lord of the <s>Mātaṅgas</s>, proclaimed the true science of the wagtail;
396.007. whether on smooth or rough ground, on uneven or even terrain, one should indicate defects and merits as stated;
396.009. the wise man should examine it there and predict its auspicious or inauspicious result.' //749//

411.003. 'The line of body-hair, the voice, the complexion, speech, and song;
411.004. the intelligence and the character — the one skilled in the treatise should examine maidens.
411.005. There, one should first examine it oneself, being discerning.' //915//

422.023. Then the <s>Brāhmaṇa</s> <s>Puṣkarasārin</s>, having ascertained the cause of their outcry and silenced them, falling down, spoke these verses to address the matter:
422.025. 'It is exactly as this <s>Triśaṅku</s> speaks;
422.026. it is truth, unfailing, real, constant, eternal, and firm.' //1068//

423.015. 'Dark and fair and likewise dusky — beings, people, some distinguished;
423.017. with bones and skin, with nails and flesh, subject to suffering and pleasure, connected with urine and excrement;
423.019. since there is no distinction of the sense-organs, therefore the four <s>varṇas</s> do not exist.' //1074//

425.001. 'Therefore, O monks, for the comprehension of the four Noble Truths that have not been comprehended, one must exert supreme effort, intense aspiration, vigor;
425.002. enthusiasm, exaltation, perseverance;
425.002. with mindfulness and clear comprehension, diligently the practice is to be undertaken.
425.003. Quickly — for the comprehension of these four Noble Truths of suffering, the origin of suffering, cessation, and the path leading to cessation — for the comprehension of these four Noble Truths that have not been comprehended, supreme and intense aspiration, vigor, exertion, enthusiasm, exaltation, perseverance, with mindfulness and clear comprehension, diligently the practice is to be undertaken.'

482.007. And he spoke a verse:
482.008. 'Free from rebirth, having abandoned dejection and pride, the <s>Buddhas</s> are the supreme cause of benefit in the world;
482.010. not without reason do the <s>Jinas</s> (Victorious Ones), who have conquered their enemies, display a smile bright as a conch shell, a lotus stalk, or white as snow.' //62//
